A||||16||Jaygee79||my car broke||||9||
Z||000000||Jaygee79||07-12-2001||09:31 AM||||Yesterday afternoon when I got home from work my car was hissing.  So I opened the trunk and it was smoking and there was water and antifreeze leaking out.  My granfather takes the thermostat out and says its fine now.  OK, so later that afternoon I leave for my other job.  I'm on my way to McDonald's for an early dinner and I notice that my voltage is a little low.  So I didn't think that much of it but I was watching it.  I get to the McDonald's speaker thing and my car is hissing again.  I look out the back window and smoke is pouring out of everywhere.  I'm thinking that my car is on fire (again).  Then the girl says, "Can I help you?"  So now I'm debating very quickly in my head, should I order a soda really quick so I have something to put the fire out with or should I check first to see if it really IS on fire?  So I choose the latter and tell the girl to hold on.  I popped the trunk and there were no flames (thank God) but water and antifreeze are spraying out all over the place and there was so much smoke I could hardly see where it was coming from (I knew where it was coming from though).  So I got back in (now the temp light is on) and drove it about 50 feet to the little parking area and went in to find a phone.  I called my grandmother and had her call work to let them know that I was going to be really really late and also to call my grandfather to come get me.  (By the way, the McDonald's girl was very nice.  She let me use her cell phone because the ^#$!@ pay phone ate my money)  So finally my grandfather gets there and by now the car has calmed down and you can see puddles of water and antifreeze all over the engine.  So my grandfather tells me to take his car to work (1981 Chevy Caprice Classic - otherwise known as the modern Titanic) and he'll take my car home.  So I (very carefully and nervously) drive his car to work.  I called him to make sure he got home with it alright and he said that he had to keep pulling over to let it cool off.  He has no clue what's wrong with it.  He said he doesn't think it's a leak, he thinks it might be a blockage or something else.  So until it's fixed I have to drive this barge everywhere I go.  I hate big cars.<P>Sorry, just had to vent.  Anybody got any ideas on whats wrong with it?  Josh will probably know, but no harm in asking.<P>------------------<BR>Jaime<BR>84SE red<BR>AOL IM: Jaygee79<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/jaygee79/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Jaygee79's Web Page</A><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pooh14.jpg"> ||||reg||9
Z||000001||rogergarrison||07-12-2001||09:39 AM||||sounds like thermostat may have stuck but dont leave them out. It prob overheated again because the water was low after the first boilover. the reason the batt lite came on could have just been anti freeze spilled on the belt. Put a new thermostat in, refill with 50/50 water and anti freeze. make sure none of the hoses are leaking. burp the system. If it happens again, may be bad radiator or water pump.||||reg||1
Z||000002||baptistheart||07-12-2001||09:39 AM||||Sounds like it's just a hose to me sometimes you can get a little pinhole in them and they look o.k. but when your car warms up the pressure causes it to spray all over. <BR>Just an educated guess mind you.<P>------------------<BR>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||07-12-2001||10:02 AM||||I'm guessing it's a bad hose or a loose clamp. I've seen leaks from both on different cars.<BR>The "smoke" you saw was probably just steam.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jake2m4||07-12-2001||11:00 AM||||This happened last time I drove mine.  Let us know what you find out or me at least.<P>------------------<BR>'85 Fiero Sport Coupe<BR>'95 Pontiac Grand AM SE||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||07-12-2001||11:09 AM||||If steam is coming out - then its gotta be coming out from somewhere - probally a leak in a hose or a loose clamp<P>  steam only escapes from the radiator cap when the pressure gets over 15 psi - which means your engine is boiling the water<P> you need to either find the leak or find out why your engine is oveheating - could be your water pump has given out on you and coolant is not pumping through the car like it should.<P>  also - DONT drive the car when its that hot - the cooling system is there for lots of good reasons - if its not working correctly you can ruin your engine.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jaygee79||07-12-2001||11:23 AM||||Don't worry, I'm not driving it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Jaime<BR>84SE red<BR>AOL IM: Jaygee79<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/jaygee79/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Jaygee79's Web Page</A><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pooh14.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||sqoach||07-12-2001||11:53 AM||||Yes, check for cracks in the hose(s) and make sure the clamps are on good. Also, check the thermostat cap. May have a bad seal causing the coolant to spew out.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forumsig.JPG">      <BR>84 SE (FOR SALE)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)(take it off my hands for $50)<BR>86 SC (FOR SALE sometime in this summer)<BR>86 SE V6 (new project)||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||07-12-2001||11:58 AM||||BTW - the coolant system is normally pressuried when the motor is hot - about 15psi<P> this acts like a pressure cooker and RAISES the boiling point of the coolant - so the engine temp can be above the normal boiling point of the coolant and it wont boil<P> but if you have a leak in the system, the pressure is lost, and the coolant will boil at a lower temp - thats why a busted hose can cause the engine to spew steam - even if there is nothing else wrong with the cooling system.<BR>  <BR>  but after it leaks for a while there wont be enought coolant left to work properly, then the engine will start to actually overheat.<P>  so to make a long reply short - it could be that all you need to do is fix the leak and refill the coolant. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||StuGood||07-12-2001||04:28 PM||||I vote for the pinhole leak theory.  I would probably start looking on the passenger (belt) side.<P>Pinhole leak leads to slipping belt(s), which reduces both alternator's output and water pump's effectiveness.  Which leads to more heat =&gt; more pressure =&gt; more leakage from the pinhole, and so forth.  A vicious circle.<P>Also might explain coolant leaking all over, temp. warning light, etc.   Pinhole leaks can be tough to locate sometimes, but not too bad to fix (you replace the hose).  The fact that coolant was leaking all over tells you there's a leak somewhere back there.  If a pressure cap was letting off steam, it'd be the one up front, on the radiator, I think.<P>Driving a barge is no fun, but a nice thing for grandma and grandpa letting you use it, eh?  Ditto for the McDonald's lady who let you use the cell phone  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!        ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||07-12-2001||05:02 PM||||Rub dry all the hoses you can, then Run it at a fast idle (have someone touch the gas up to ~ 3000rpm), while cold. The pressure from the pump should cause even a small leak to sweat, or drip. Also, make sure the filler cap is closed tightly. I just dealt with this last night  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">. Check the connections at the filler, and at the waterpump. It it's blowing steam, it's either overheating big time, or splashing on the manifolds. This suggests that the leak is near the top.<BR>If none of the hoses leak, there are 2 more possibilities:<P>1) the minor one: Check that the block heater core hasen't blown out it's seal. Follow the power cable to it's place on the block. Mine's done this and caused all your problems listed. Mine also fell out. go figure  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>2) THE BIG ONE: Blown head gasket. Look for coolan coming out of the block itself. This is unlikely, as you said the car seems fine otherwise, and a bad gasket usually brings all sorts of other problems out to play.||||reg||3
Z||000011||88CoupeV6||07-12-2001||07:09 PM||||I just fixed it. The rubber under the thermostat housing cap ripped and broke allowing all the coolant to squirt out when the car heated up. It was like a pressure cooker.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue">sqoach was correct.</FONT><P>------------------<BR>Josh <B>(known by TheFiero64 for first 1239 posts)</B><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">    <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||7
Z||000012||88CoupeV6||07-12-2001||07:10 PM||||after reading all the posts you were all somewhat right. thanks everyone  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000013||sqoach||07-12-2001||08:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88CoupeV6:<BR><B>I just fixed it. The rubber under the thermostat housing cap ripped and broke allowing all the coolant to squirt out when the car heated up. It was like a pressure cooker.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue">sqoach was correct.</FONT><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yay! I feel like I've actually been helpful to someone now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Glad you got it fixed. I remember my thermostat cap gasket was pretty crappy, so I replaced that cap.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Jaygee79||07-13-2001||12:35 AM||||I went to Autozone to get a new one.  I looked all over the place for them but didn't see them.  Finally I asked the guy at the counter.  He seemed slightly perturbed &lt;sp&gt; that I bothered him.  He asked what car and what year.  Then he looked at the computer and said "Don't have it, can't get it.  Try Foreign Auto."  Yeah, OK.||||reg||1
